  "interpretation": "## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 4,199 people used the product. Weekly users dipped from 818 to 323 in September, then climbed to 1,241 by the week of November 8. In the latest 14 days, people rose 21.32% and actions rose 28.71%. Actions growing faster than people suggests each person did more. Estimated work grew only 19.47%, so the extra actions may have been lighter tasks.\n\nUpgrades came quickly. Release 2.6.12 reached 53.36% of people in its second week, though 12.3% still used the older 2.0.7 the week before.\n\n## How people use it\n\nReading and writing cell values are the most common actions, followed by formulas. Tables and ranges make up 65.31% of actions. Share of work is an estimate, not time or speed: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3, heavy data work counts 10. By that estimate, macros are 6.74% of actions but 13.7% of work, and Power Query data imports are 3.1% versus 8.42%.\n\nSince late September, 530 command line users averaged 1,441.5 actions each, while 5,132 AI assistant users averaged 276.8. These are different people over a shorter window. Command line use may lean toward cell editing (77.75% of actions), while assistant use seems more spread out.\n\nIn the last 30 days, a typical assistant session had 13 actions, and 71.64% used more than one area. Sessions over 200 actions were 4.74% of sessions but 46.86% of actions. Of new people, 47.77% came back after a week and 34.22% after three weeks; 56.7% of all people returned at least once. Weekdays averaged 47,283 actions versus 24,372 on weekends. Many tried advanced areas on day one, such as 81.01% for Power Query. Typical waits were 1.2 seconds for Power Query and 0.13 seconds for tables. Waits depend on workbook size and computer, so they do not score quality.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nGitHub release files lead with 33,380 downloads, then npm MCP Server with 19,611, VS Code installs with 10,275, and npm command line with 8,746. npm totals cover the last 12 months; the others count since each channel started. Downloads are not people: one person can download several times, updates and automated installs add to totals, and channels overlap, so they should not be added together.\n\nWeekly npm downloads peaked at 7,459 in the week of October 25 and were 2,338 in the latest week. Release 2.6.11 had 1,281 downloads and 2.6.12 had 1,206. The biggest weekly gain for GitHub and VS Code was 3,285 from October 26; the latest week added 1,730.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make heavy data imports and macros easier to follow, since they carry a large share of estimated work. We will help new people find a second reason to return. We will encourage people on older releases to upgrade.",
